    "reading": {
        "body": "New Mexico's 2026 legislative session produced a dense cluster of AI bills, but they are mostly transparency, accountability, and chatbot safety measures, not laws governing compute, data centers, or power.\n\nThe bill list is dominated by AI governance: the Artificial Intelligence Accountability Act, the Artificial Intelligence Government Use Act, the Artificial Intelligence Transparency Act, the Chatbot Safety Act, and a bill making AI ethics a school science elective. Alongside these sit a Data Broker Privacy Act, a Consumer Information and Data Protection Act, and a Voice and Visual Likeness Rights Act. The only infrastructure-adjacent item is a Quantum Facility Infrastructure Tax Credit, which concerns quantum rather than AI compute. Nothing in the list addresses data center construction, electricity supply, or grid interconnection.\n\nThe news items point the same direction. New Mexico is seeking up to $40 billion from Meta in a data privacy case, the attorney general and a state lawmaker announced a push to rein in rogue AI models, and a jury found Facebook deceived users over data privacy. These are enforcement and liability actions about data and model behavior, not about the physical buildout of AI.\n\nWhat this data does not show is any New Mexico activity on the binding constraint this site tracks. There is no bill on power procurement for data centers, no transmission or generation measure tied to AI load, and no siting or water legislation framed around compute. The water items that do appear concern Taos specifically and are not linked to AI in the data. The reader should treat New Mexico as a state legislating AI at the model and consumer-protection layer while the compute and power layer is absent from its 2026 record here.",
